1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:06,600
At the center of every customer account is one person.

2
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:17,900
Meetings, messages, support, consumption, delivery, risk… each living in its own system. Keeping it all connected quietly consumes much of a CSAM’s week.

3
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:29,600
Step back, and the problem becomes clear. The information isn’t disconnected. The operating model is.

4
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:41,900
Not a chatbot. Not another automation. Magister is a persistent intelligence layer that understands the account, maintains context, and notices when something changes.

5
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:49,300
Specialized agents take ownership of specific responsibilities—and continuously monitor them over time.

6
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:57,400
Traditional automation follows a script. It’s triggered, it runs, and it stops.

7
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:06,600
An agent owns a responsibility. It observes, understands, acts—and keeps watching.

8
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:20,800
The account no longer has to be reconstructed from memory every morning. Commitments are tracked. Changes are investigated. Risks surface before someone remembers to look for them.

9
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:32,100
And when something genuinely requires human judgment, it comes to the CSAM—with the context already assembled.

10
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:47,500
That changes the job itself. Less time maintaining the account. More time with customer leaders, navigating difficult conversations, making decisions, and driving outcomes.

11
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:11,100
So the real question isn’t how much faster AI can make the CSAM.
